  "worked_solution": "We wish to find the limits of integration ${u}_{1}(y,z)$ and ${u}_{2}(y,z)$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x/4}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x/4-z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x={\u222c}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type III region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 4,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1-\\frac{x}{4},\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1-\\frac{x}{4}-z}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}A,$ we write $R$ as a type I region. \n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $z$-axis and in the direction of increasing $z,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/dc1a9b6371456f635cd4b73fe99301fa349ceeec1341b9bd3b86f6bcd7ad8ba2.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the bottom boundary and leaves through the top boundary:\n\n- The top boundary of the region, written in the form $z=z(x,y),$ is\n\n$$\ny=1-\\frac{x}{4}-z\\,\u27f9\\,z=1-\\frac{x}{4}-y.\n$$\n\n- The bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nz=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type I representation of $R$ is \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1-\\frac{x}{4}-y},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/07c4ee04dfba186348b3bd56b182477cec9ca1a581e37bb0eb4ab9a3d64ee515.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration so that it is integrated first with respect to $z$ and then over $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x/4}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x/4-z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x & ={\u222c}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-x/4-y}f(x,y,z)\\,dz]dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
